course: dictionary
lesson: 962
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Blowing has more than one meaning. The words around it tell you which meaning is right. «Blowing out candles» means pushing air out of your mouth. «The wind is blowing» means air is moving strongly. It is the same word doing different jobs.
explain: Read the whole sentence before you choose. «Blowing your nose» means pushing air through your nose into a tissue, while «blowing a whistle» means making a sound by pushing air into it. Ask yourself: what is being blown, and where does the air go?
explain: Some phrases are not about air at all. «Whistle-blowing» means telling someone in charge about wrongdoing in a group you belong to. Do not guess from the word alone. Put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Blowing is the -ing form of blow, so it can describe something happening right now.
fact: «Blowing out a candle» pushes air out of your mouth, which moves the flame's hot gas away from the wick.
fact: «The wind is blowing from the north» tells you the direction the air is moving from.
fact: «Blowing bubbles» and «blowing a whistle» both mean pushing air into something.
fact: «Whistle-blowing» means telling someone in charge about wrongdoing in a group you belong to.
exercise: «Blowing out a candle pushes the flame's hot gas away from the wick.» What does blowing mean here? | Moving a toy boat across a pond | *Pushing air out of your mouth | Playing a loud note on a horn | Telling someone about wrongdoing
exercise: «The wind is blowing from the north.» What does blowing tell you about the wind? | The wind is making a whistling sound | The wind is asleep and has stopped | The wind is too cold to move | *Air is moving strongly from the north
exercise: «A camel can close its nostrils against blowing sand.» What is the sand doing? | Being sucked up by the camel | *Being moved along by the wind | Sitting still in a pile | Turning into water
test: Which phrase uses blowing to mean making a sound by pushing air into something? | *Blowing a whistle | Blowing your nose | Blowing out candles | Blowing sand across the beach
test: Tom told the head teacher that his group was cheating on the test. What is this called? | Blowing up a balloon | *Whistle-blowing | Blowing bubbles | Blowing out candles
exam: Which meaning fits «The leaves were blowing across the yard»? | Pushing air through your nose into a tissue | Making a sound by pushing air into a horn | *Being moved along as air moves strongly | Telling someone in charge about wrongdoing
